What is Abstract Factory?

Abstract Factory is a creational design pattern that provides an interface for
creating families of related or dependent objects without specifying their concrete
classes.

===================
There are two important words:

Factory → responsible for creating objects.
Abstract → the client works with abstractions rather than concrete implementations.

Factory Method → creates ONE type of product.
Abstract Factory → creates a FAMILY of related products.
=====================


The critical difference from Factory Method is:

Factory Method generally focuses on creating one product, while Abstract Factory
creates a family of related products.

=======================

Why do we need Abstract Factory?

Imagine you're developing an application that supports two cloud providers:

AWS
Azure

Your application needs three infrastructure components:

Database
Storage
Message Queue

For AWS:

AWS
├── AWS Database
├── AWS Storage
└── AWS Queue

For Azure:

Azure
├── Azure Database
├── Azure Storage
└── Azure Queue

The important requirement is:

If the application chooses AWS, all infrastructure components should be AWS-compatible.

Similarly:

If the application chooses Azure, all infrastructure components should be Azure-compatible.

This is a family of related objects.

That's where Abstract Factory becomes useful.


                    CloudFactory
                         |
            +------------+------------+
            |                         |
       AWSFactory                AzureFactory
            |                         |
      +-----+-----+             +-----+-----+
      |     |     |             |     |     |
      ↓     ↓     ↓             ↓     ↓     ↓
     DB   Storage Queue        DB   Storage Queue


==============================
The four major participants

Abstract Factory usually has these components:

1. Abstract Factory
2. Concrete Factory
3. Abstract Products
4. Concrete Products

===============================

Advantages of Abstract Factory
1. Supports product families
This is its biggest strength.

2. Ensures product compatibility
Related objects can be created consistently.

3. Reduces coupling
Client doesn't know concrete implementations.

4. Supports dependency inversion
Clients depend on abstractions.

5. Easy to switch environments/providers

=============================

Factory Method
      ↓
Individual creation method

Abstract Factory
      ↓
Collection of creation methods
      ↓
Creates a related product family

===========================

| Pattern          | Main purpose                        |
| ---------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Singleton        | Ensure one instance                 |
| Factory Method   | Delegate creation of a product      |
| Abstract Factory | Create a family of related products |
